 "seat": {
  "BEST_WHY": "The floor is genuinely built rather than tiled. In the lit area below the figure the slab joints are dark dashed pixel runs whose weight and continuity *vary along a single run* \u2014 the horizontal joint at roughly y\u2248680 breaks and re-forms around x\u2248600, and slab sizes are irregular (half-slabs at the left edge, a doubled slab under the figure, a noticeably paler replacement stone at ~x430\u2013520,y560\u2013600 that reads as a stone swapped in later). The crack lines are hand-drawn continuous curves that cross slab boundaries and ignore the grid entirely \u2014 the long arc running from ~(60,600) down to (450,760) passes straight over four joints. That is how cracks actually propagate through masonry, and it is the single strongest signal in the deck that a person drew this rather than stamped it. The corridor mouth at x\u2248390\u2013440 is also correctly built: the corridor floor holds warm tan and its own joint pattern, and it terminates against wall runs on both sides that read as separate objects, not as a recolour of the floor.",
  "WORST_WHY": "Three things, any one of which is disqualifying.\n\nFirst, there are no walls. The dirt field, the grey flagstone at left and right, and the water are all coplanar floor recolours that simply abut. The dirt-to-flagstone boundary at x\u224856 and again at x\u2248344 is a pixel-straight vertical cut, one pixel wide, with no wall thickness, no worn edge, no dirt spilling onto the stone. The frame is a painted tilemap, not a room.\n\nSecond, the water at x\u2248250\u2013344, y\u22480\u2013190 is a flat rectangle. I measured it: **7 unique colours across the whole body, standard deviation 2.64**. It is a flat teal fill with a stamped hexagonal cell pattern over it, terminating in hard 90\u00b0 corners with zero edge treatment \u2014 no bank, no lip, no darker shallows, no shore. It reads as a selection filled with a colour.\n\nThird, the two crates on the left at x64\u201396 \u2014 the one at y0\u201332 and the one at y48\u201380 \u2014 are **byte-identical, maximum pixel difference 0**. Same lid highlight, same latch, same diamond keyhole, in the same column, 48px apart. So are the two dark bodies beneath them. The teal moss blobs are likewise the same three or four shapes reused at left and right. And nothing in the frame is lit or grounded: no object casts a contact shadow, the two barrels at the bottom sit on the dirt with no shadow at all, and the whole scene is at flat uniform ambient.",
  "FLAGGED": "1, 2",
  "RANK": "3 > 2 > 1",
