 "flip_list_withheld": [
  "The darkness is doing two incompatible things in the same frame. There is a smooth radial falloff *and* hard-edged axis-aligned black rectangles: a pixel-crisp vertical black edge at x\u2248180 running from y0 to y\u2248135, and a horizontal one at y\u2248134 running right to x\u2248500, plus a second black rectangle x\u224860\u2013520, y\u2248268\u2013650. Those hard rectangle boundaries cut straight across wall and floor alike. Either feather every one of those edges into the radial falloff, or commit to hard tile-quantised occlusion everywhere and drop the smooth gradient \u2014 but not both in one frame.",
  "The lit core is the least-detailed part of the picture. In the 60px band around the figure the floor is a flat cream/peach field with no surface information inside the slabs at all. The light should reveal the most detail where it is strongest; here it erases it. Pull the lit-zone highlight down about 30 luminance and re-introduce the chisel texture and joint variation into the brightest slabs.",
  "Joints in the lit zone are *lighter* than the slab faces \u2014 the vertical joint immediately left of the figure at x\u2248365 is a pale pink line. A joint is a recess; it cannot be brighter than the stone it separates. Invert those so every joint sits below its neighbouring faces at every light level.",
  "There are dozens of orphaned joint stubs: short dark vertical bars that start and stop inside a slab and connect to nothing. Clear examples at (620,490), (640,530), (690,520), (655,650), (620,650). A joint separates two stones; these separate nothing. Delete every stub that does not terminate at another joint or at a wall.",
  "The olive-brown rectangle at x\u2248235\u2013305, y\u2248650\u2013715 is a hard-edged untextured box in a hue family that appears nowhere else, with no top/side distinction and no shadow. It reads as a placeholder. Either texture it as an actual object with a lit top face, a darker side face and a cast shadow, or remove it.",
  "There is a straight-line shadow wedge running diagonally from the upper-left of the lower floor area down toward (350,850) that corresponds to no occluder in the scene. Delete it or place the geometry that would produce it.",
  "The wall surfaces are soft blurred mottle with crisp 1px pixel marks drawn on top \u2014 two incompatible resolutions in the same surface, most visible along the wall top at y\u2248140\u2013265 in the upper area. That blur is the strongest \"a filter was run over this\" signal in the frame. Quantise the wall texture to the same pixel grid as the joint marks so the whole surface resolves at one scale.",
  "The wall's bottom edge against the floor at y\u2248455 is a hard 1px line with no shadow cast onto the floor below it. Add a 3\u20134px darker band on the floor along the base of every wall run.",
  "Same repeated diagonal hatch stamp as in 3, at higher density across the right-hand floor. Vary or thin it."
